Issue: http.agent options creates different name when used with a request.

Problem:
This is a rehash of: https://github.com/nodejs/node-v0.x-archive/pull/7792

Consider the following code:

```
const https = require('https');
const agent = new https.Agent({ rejectUnauthorized: false });
const options = {
  hostname: 'encrypted.google.com',
  port: 443,
  path: '/',
  method: 'GET',
  agent: agent,
};

const req = https.request(options, (r) => { r.pipe(process.stdout); });
req.end();
```

If we take a look at `agent.sockets`, we see something like:

```
{ 'encrypted.google.com:443:::::::': [] }
```

This name should have been removed by https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/master/lib/_http_agent.js#L225, but was not. 

It is not removed because `Agent.prototype.createSocket` extends options before it calls `getName`, `Agent.prototype.addRequest` does not. Because we have `{rejectUnauthorized: false}` being passed via options to `new Agent`, it generates a name with `false` at the end. This gets removed correctly. 

The workaround in this case is to set the option in the request instead, but ideally we should normalize all name generation to create consistent keys.

